  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

This is a model of consumption in presence of "complex behavior". Complex behavior arises when agents interact with each other, such that consumption of each agent is affected by that of his or her neighbors or peers. 

The theory and motivation behind the model is explained in Al-Suwailem (2008) and (2011).

## HOW IT WORKS

Consumption is modeled as a function of income and net-wealth. In addition, consumption of agents is affected by the average consumption of their neighbors. Accordingly, each agent prefers to choose consumption level close to his neighbors. However, if income is insufficient to finance the desired level of consumption, the agent needs to obtain external financing.

There are three modes of finance that the model provides:

1. Interest-free lending.
2. Interest-based lending.
3. Markup financing

Interest-free lending does not generate income to lenders. Therefore it does not affect consumption behavior of lenders.

Interest-based lending generates income to lenders. This affects lenders' consumption level, since higher income leads to higher consumption. As consumption of lenders rises, the average level of consumption of the group also rises. As average consumption rises, neighboring agents want to raise their consumption as well, because they want to conform to their neighbors. But this means they need to finance the higher level of consumption. 

The second round of financing will generate additional income to lenders, which leads to a third round of financing, etc. Deficit agents, i.e. agents that need to finance their desired consumption, will fall into a viscous circle of borrowing. The circle stops only with bankruptcy, when amount-due exceeds the average income of the agent, whereby the agent is declared bankrupt.

Markup financing finances only actual consumption, and does not allow for refinancing of previous debt. The income generated by financing (the markup) causes the consumption level of creditors to rise. But the vicious circle is blocked since no loans are allowed to finance previous debt. Financing is extended only for real consumption.

The overall results of the model suggests that:

1.    Markup financing is fundamentally different from interest or riba financing. Despite some apparent similarities, they have starkly different impacts on economic variables. 

2.    Interest is not merely a price; it is a mechanism for debt growth. This is not the case for markup financing. Markup restricts debt to actual consumption, thus controlling the growth of debt to that of available resources. Interest in contrast allows debt to grow exponentially. Debt growth in this case is controlled not by interest but through bankruptcy.

3.    Delayed payments are treated quite differently in the two models. In the interest model, they represent a source of income to lenders. This leads to very low turnover of funds, explosion of debt and consequently to economic cycles. The markup model does not allow for generating profits from these obligations. This puts an incentive to creditors not to extend credit beyond a level that would permit for delay to arise in the first place. 

4.    The interest model demonstrates several undesirable results. These include: cycles and instability, wealth concentration, low fund utilization and thus high inefficiency, and high costs of borrowing. In contrast, the markup model shows stability, high efficiency, less concentration of wealth, and relatively low costs of financing.

5.    The markup model is slightly more concentrated than the standard model, but it shows higher savings and liquidity. Although not modeled at this stage, higher savings potentially promote higher investment and higher economic growth.
 

Charity

When consumption is affected by that of neighbors, charity have a qualitiatively different impact. Transfers from high net-worth agents to low net-worth ones, might improve consumption of both, donors and receivers. 


## HOW TO USE IT

You need to decide which mode of finance you want to explore.

1. For interest-free model, set r = 0, and m = 0 in the interface. This prevents any interest (r) or markup (m) to be charged.

2. For interest-based model, set r to any positive number, for example 6% p.a. Be sure to set m = 0. You cannot have both m and r positive.

3. For markup-model, set m = 6% p.a., or any number you choose. Be sure that "limit-amount-due" button is on. 

This button protects creditors in the markup model from extending too much credit to consumers, for which they do not get any returns. An important difference between markup and interest-based models is that delay in paying amount due can be charged for in the interest model, but not in the markup model. Islamic finance does not allow creditors to make any profits from delay of payment. So creditors in markup model will lose money if agents do not pay on time.

To avoid delay of payment, the amount-due is capped by 50% of average income of the agent. In this manner, the amount-due will never exceed income, and therefore no past-dues will arise.


Relative Behavior

You also need to decide to what extend an agent is affected by the behavior of his neighbors. This is controlled by the "relativity" slide in the interface. A relativity = 
0.5 means that 50% of desired consumption is decided by income and wealth, while 50% is decided by the average consumption of neighbors. Try relativity = 0.5, and see how economic behavior is affected by the three modes of finance.

Immediately beneath the relativity slide is a slide for the standard deviation of the relativity parameter. That is, by how much agents get above or below the set value. If std-r = 30%, and relativity = .5, then gents will have a relativity value of 0.5 plus or minus 30% of the 0.5. So the range of relativity will be [0.1, 0.65].


Income

You may choose income from the Income slider. Choose the standard deviation from the std-income from the slider immediately beneath it. The chosen income level will be the average income across agents. For each agent, however, his income will be normally distributed, with mean = income, and standard deviation = std. The "std" is set in the slider beneath.

Marginal propensity of consumption out of income is set in the income-propensity slider. The marginal propensity out of wealth is set in the wealth-propensity slider. The standard deviation for each is immediately beneath it.


Loan Period

You can decide the length of financing in the loan-period slider. Think of each period as a month. 60 periods is equivalent to 5 years. The interest rate and markup rate are calculated based on assuming each period to be one month.


Delay Time

This slider decides for how long a bankrupt agent will stay "unemployed". The longer the period, the greater is the drop in aggregate income and consumption.


Allow-lending

This button gives the user the ability to allow or not to allow for lending of any kind. This might be helpful to control forthe effect of charity/donation on consumption behavior.


z

This is the parameter used for charity/donation. z is a percentage value to be deducted from agents with higher net-worth than average. This is donation given by the rich to the needy. For the receiver, it is recorded as charity. To examine the impact of donation/charity, it is better to turn lending off, i.e. set "allow-lending" button off.


Patches Screen

The patches screen shows the consumption level of each agent (patch). The darker the color, the lower is the level of consumption. Red patches reflect bankrupt agents.


Graphs

All graphs have annotated explanations within.


Consistency Checks

To check the consistency of the model, the following conditions are examined:

1. Total sources of funds must equal total uses of the funds. Hence, 
   income + loan + charity - consumption - surplus = zero,

2. Total shares of agents in accumulated surplus must be equal to one.

3. Aggregate net wealth of the economy is simply total cash, since debt and credit cancel each other out.

4. Total wealth equals total cash + total credit.
 

## THINGS TO NOTICE AND TO TRY

You can compare different modes of finance in various dimensions. 

For example, compare cost of financing in interest-based lending and markup financing. Which one is higher? Why?

Compare turnover rate. Which one is higher? What does it mean?

Interest-based model uses compound interest (interest on unpaid interest). Try using simple interest on;y. Can you figure out how to change it? What impact does it have?


## EXTENDING THE MODEL

There are many ways to improve and extend the model.

For example, income is exogenous to the model. It can be made endogenous by modelling investment.

Financing is made centrally in the model. It can be made locally, i.e. for neighbors only.


## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

The author acknowledges the support of NetLogo group discussions.

The Lorenz curve and Gini Index graphs are taken from NetLogo Models Library, Wealth Distribution, (c) Uri Wilensky.

Original papers are:

Al-Suwailem, Sami (2008) Islamic Economics in a Complex World, Islamic Development Bank.

Al-Suwailem, Sami (2011) Behavioural Complexity, Journal of Economic Surveys, vol. 25.

See also:

Al-Suwailem, Sami (2013) Simulation in Islamic Economics, presentation made at Mohammadia School of Engineering.</info>
